import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"kit-term-bridge — mlopt terms emitted as .hnl, evaluated through CatoptKit","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/kit-term-bridge.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/kit-term-bridge.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/kit-term-bridge.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="kit-term-bridge-—-mlopt-terms-emitted-as-hnl-evaluated-through-catoptkit" tabindex="-1">kit-term-bridge — mlopt terms emitted as <code>.hnl</code>, evaluated through CatoptKit <a class="header-anchor" href="#kit-term-bridge-—-mlopt-terms-emitted-as-hnl-evaluated-through-catoptkit" aria-label="Permalink to “kit-term-bridge — mlopt terms emitted as .hnl, evaluated through CatoptKit”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Date:</strong> 2026-10-20 · <strong>Surface:</strong> <code>packages/mlopt-kit/src/bin/hnl-run.rs</code> (+ <code>tests/cli.rs</code>), <code>lab/kit/term_hnl.py</code>, <code>tests/test_kit_bridge.py</code> · <strong>Gates:</strong> <code>cargo test -p mlopt-kit</code> — <strong>21/21 green</strong>; <code>pytest tests/test_kit_bridge.py</code> — <strong>8/8 green</strong> (marked <code>e2e</code>: the suite crosses the external engine boundary).</p><h2 id="what-landed" tabindex="-1">What landed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-landed" aria-label="Permalink to “What landed”">​</a></h2><p>The frontier after <code>mlopt-kit</code> (retro <code>mlopt-kit.md</code>) was the second half of &quot;the e-graph&#39;s terms and the kit&#39;s runtime are the same program space&quot;: mlopt&#39;s optimization machinery emitting/consuming programs through the kit, not just hand-written <code>.hnl</code>. Three pieces, each thin:</p><ul><li><strong><code>hnl-run</code></strong> — a subprocess door over <code>CatoptKit</code>: <code>.hnl</code> in on stdin or a file path, the <code>serde_json::Value</code> boundary form out (<code>{&quot;__tensor__&quot;: …}</code> for a tensor). <code>--project &lt;dir&gt;</code> wires a <code>.handl.toml</code>; <code>--repeat N</code> evaluates the source N times inside one kit — the measurement hook a costed decision needs (<code>(t(N) − t(1))/(N−1)</code> wall per call). Deliberately <em>program-generic</em> — nothing term-aware — so emitted terms and the <code>hnl/</code> modules go through the same door.</li><li><strong><code>lab/kit/term_hnl.py</code></strong> — the emit/eval bridge. <code>emit</code> renders any term as a <code>tensor.*</code> expression from a <strong>data table</strong> (<code>_OpSpec</code> per op: <code>slots</code> assemble the call; <code>accepts</code> pins attrs the surface can honour — <code>conv2d</code> has no dilation slot, <code>batch_norm</code> has no <code>training=True</code>; <code>drop</code> ignores the eval-inert <code>momentum</code>/ <code>cudnn_enabled</code>; <code>arity</code> picks the signature by operand count — <code>batch_norm</code>&#39;s export variants synthesize the identity affine the op requires). <code>emit_program</code> binds <code>Var</code>/<code>Param</code> leaves in a <code>do</code>- prelude: <code>{&quot;shape&quot;,&quot;data&quot;}</code> → <code>reshape(from_list(flat))</code>, <code>{&quot;hnl&quot;}</code> → a verbatim expression (the marshaling channel for <em>computed</em> inputs — the fold&#39;s <code>s</code>). <code>KitRunner</code> shells <code>hnl-run</code> and decodes the wire form.</li><li><strong><code>tests/test_kit_bridge.py</code></strong> — the verification, three claims.</li></ul><h2 id="verified" tabindex="-1">Verified <a class="header-anchor" href="#verified" aria-label="Permalink to “Verified”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>A real artifact end to end.</strong> <code>TorchSource().to_ir</code> of an eval-mode <code>Conv2d → BatchNorm2d</code> — the actual <code>aten.conv2d</code> + <code>aten.batch_norm</code> IR with its full attr tail — emits as <code>tensor.batch_norm(tensor.conv2d(x, p_conv_weight, p_conv_bias, [1,1], [1,1]), …, 0.00001)</code> and evaluates through the kit to the torch-fp64 answer (<strong>max abs diff 6.7e-16</strong> on a seeded fixture).</li><li><strong>A law&#39;s two forms through the runtime.</strong> <code>conv_channel_scale</code> (the <code>CONV_FOLD_RULES</code> anchor) applied via <code>meta.apply_rewrite_at</code>: <code>mul(conv2d(x,W,b), sv)</code> and its <code>conv2d(x, W∘sv′, b∘sv)</code> rewrite both emit and evaluate <strong>dyadic-exact-equal</strong> — and <code>dag_cost(flops_cost)</code> credits the fold (the absorbed <code>mul</code>/<code>reshape</code> are param-only subtrees — compile-time work).</li><li><strong>An optimization decision costed through the runtime.</strong> The conv+BN fold as a decision: spelled <code>batch_norm(conv2d(x,W,b), g, be, m, v, eps)</code> vs deployed <code>conv2d(x, W2, b2)</code> — where <code>W2</code>/<code>b2</code> are <em>mlopt subterms</em> (<code>mul(w, reshape(s,(C,1,1,1)))</code>, <code>add(mul(b,s), sub(be,mul(s,m)))</code>) materialized through the kit itself. Deployed == unfolded (&lt; 1e-12), and the measured per-call cost is <strong>82.1 ms vs 95.4 ms</strong> (<code>--repeat 30</code>, debug build) with <code>dag_cost</code> agreeing directionally (<strong>36864 vs 55296</strong>). The fold&#39;s value lands where it should — in the deployed program.</li><li><strong>Generality + the honest bound.</strong> A matmul/add/sub/reshape/transpose/concat term evaluates against the torch reference; <code>pack</code> emits a multi-output list; <code>sigmoid</code>, <code>rsqrt</code>, and training-mode <code>batch_norm</code> each refuse by name.</li></ol><h2 id="the-precisely-named-missing-surface" tabindex="-1">The precisely-named missing surface <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-precisely-named-missing-surface" aria-label="Permalink to “The precisely-named missing surface”">​</a></h2><p>The one piece of the conv+BN fold the bridge cannot emit as a <em>term</em>: <code>s = γ·rsqrt(σ²+ε)</code> — <strong>elementwise unary ops are absent from host-tensor&#39;s 18-op surface</strong> (<code>rsqrt</code>/<code>sqrt</code>/<code>neg</code>/<code>exp</code>/<code>sigmoid</code>, and the reductions <code>sum</code>/<code>mean</code>/<code>max</code>). The spelled RHS of the fold composite dies on <code>rsqrt</code> first (<code>UnsupportedOpError(&quot;rsqrt&quot;)</code>, tested). Workaround exercised here: <code>s</code> rides the <code>{&quot;hnl&quot;}</code> marshaling channel — the <code>(C,)</code> stats materialize to lists and scalar <code>**</code> finishes each channel, <code>hnl/conv_bn.hnl</code>&#39;s own escape. The honest fix is a host-tensor op addition (elementwise unaries), not bridge machinery.</p><h2 id="findings-worth-carrying" tabindex="-1">Findings worth carrying <a class="header-anchor" href="#findings-worth-carrying" aria-label="Permalink to “Findings worth carrying”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The handl lexer does not read scientific notation.</strong> <code>1e-05</code> lexes as the token sequence <code>1</code> <code>e</code> <code>-05</code> — <em>silently</em> evaluating to <code>1</code> in statement position (measured: <code>e :: 1e-05</code> bound <code>e = 1</code>), and failing inside a list. A mlopt <code>eps=1e-05</code> emitted naively would silently become <code>eps=1</code>. <code>_num</code> expands every float through <code>Decimal</code> (<code>1e-05</code> → <code>0.00001</code>), and the emitted-source test pins the spelling. This is an upstream lexer gap worth a handl-side fix.</li><li><strong><code>dag_cost</code>&#39;s param-only discount models compile-time folding the kit runtime does not perform</strong> — the emitted program evaluates <code>mul(w, reshape(sv))</code> per call. That is why the fold-decision test separates <em>compute</em> (the fold, run once) from <em>deploy</em> (folded weights as leaf data): the per-call measurement then prices what the cost model prices. Reported honestly: through the bridge, an unfolded <code>mul(conv,sv)</code> is <em>cheaper per call</em> than its folded RHS spelled as runtime work.</li><li><strong>Bound names</strong>: torch-export leaf names are dotted (<code>conv.weight</code>) and dots are field access in handl — <code>_sanitize</code> maps to <code>conv_weight</code>; <code>tensor</code> itself is reserved (binding it would shadow the op namespace).</li><li>The upstream <code>hnl</code> CLI (<code>host-cli</code>) is <strong>not</strong> a usable door: it panics in <code>plugin-engine-typecheck-handl</code> on a trivial program and, more fundamentally, has no <code>host-tensor</code> <code>OpRegistry</code> linked — the <code>tensor.*</code> ops are Rust impls only <code>CatoptKit</code> wires. That is why <code>hnl-run</code> lives in <code>mlopt-kit</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="what-remains-open-named" tabindex="-1">What remains open (named) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-remains-open-named" aria-label="Permalink to “What remains open (named)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The e-graph → <code>.hnl</code> loop is one direction deep.</strong> The bridge emits terms <em>into</em> the kit; nothing yet lifts a <code>TaggedValue::Tensor</code> or an <code>.hnl</code> program <em>back</em> into IR (the <code>OnnxSource</code>/<code>OnnxSink</code> precedent says the seam shape is a <code>Source</code>/<code>Sink</code> pair — a <code>KitSink</code> implementing <code>ports.Sink.lower/verify</code> is the natural next step).</li><li><strong>Promotion floor.</strong> <code>lab/kit/term_hnl.py</code> sits in <code>lab/</code> under the 99%-coverage promotion rule; its tests are <code>e2e</code>-marked, so the dev lane (<code>-m &quot;not slow&quot;</code>) runs them but <code>-m &quot;not e2e&quot;</code> does not.</li><li><code>--repeat</code> timing is wall-clock in a debug build — directionally honest, not a benchmark rig.</li></ul>`,13)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};